title dawk answers the crux, and it answers no
prompt kg -- fetch dawk into gratitude/ first. (And mid-turn: pheromone's lap could not land.)
think Read the license from its own bytes rather than a summary, which is what the gratitude rule asks. MIT, verbatim, Copyright 2024-2025 triallax. Held as a gitlink submodule, whole and unmodified, catalogued with source and pin -- the convention this room already keeps for tigerbeetle, zig, sixos and the rest.
obs A public summary called the repository 83 percent C++. Its own bytes say src/ is 44 files, 42 of them Zig, 8,293 lines; the C++ is a vendored simdutf. I nearly carried the summary into a gratitude page and into a fetch order. The correction is recorded on the page itself, because the difference between repeating a summary and reading a source is the whole of that room.
think Then the crux, and it was answerable on metal rather than by argument: dawk wants Zig 0.16.0 and our vendored toolchain IS 0.16.0. One zig build, no second toolchain, a working binary.
obs And the answer is NO, which is the cheap answer the fetch order was designed to buy first. Asked to run our own tools/fixtures/l/utf8_valid.awk -- a validator whose entire job is to find bytes that are not valid UTF-8 -- dawk says encountered invalid UTF-8 and exits 1. It is Unicode-native and consistent about it: length of a four-letter word ending in a two-byte character reads 4 under dawk and 5 under gawk in the C locale.
obs So the re-grow spec is not a POSIX awk. It is a POSIX awk that keeps the C-locale byte view, because a tool that inspects bytes cannot be built on a reader that insists its input is already text. That requirement had never been written down anywhere in this tree, and it took someone else's honest program refusing it to surface. The first program we would have needed to port is precisely the one that cannot run.
obs That is not a fault in dawk and the gratitude page says so plainly. It is a young learning project whose author warns here be dragons, and it taught us more by refusing than a passing test would have.
think Pheromone's lap arrived mid-round having done real work it could not land, and its diagnosis was correct on all four counts. I read its tree, which the captain's hat allows, and did not touch it, which the same sentence requires.
obs The fault in the shared tree is birth_a_clone: it copies four signing values from the field and asserts each git config call succeeded. git config user.name "" SUCCEEDS. So a field carrying nothing seats nothing and four asserts read ok -- an inherited value is not a checked value, and a successful call is not a seated one. The fourth value was worse for being non-empty: gpg.program is an absolute path into the field's tree, grain-harvest became grain-petrichor on 20260904, and tracked_link_scan cannot reach an untracked .git/config.
obs GATE 3 and I stopped there. .gnupg-rye/ holds private-keys-v1.d/. Letting pheromone sign means placing secret key material into a third directory, which is Keaton's hand alone. The structural half rides with it: the one-field design assumes every clone can read one directory, and the enclosure gives each tree only itself, so per-tree GNUPGHOME is the only shape that works jailed -- a custody decision rather than an engineering one.
obs A live derived-spine collision, and the law settled it without argument. Pheromone holds unshared %416 at 07:06 and %417 at 07:18; my %416 at 07:21 is already published to xy. Published never moves, unshared renumbers, so its two become %418 and %419 on rebase. Its stamps are earlier and it still renumbers, which is the law preferring one order over one hand's precedence.
obs Two of my own to own. I swept 71 lines of elder prose in gratitude/README.md while fixing my own two lines of non-ASCII -- reverted, and only my entry re-applied. And a card edit inserted rather than replaced, duplicating a whole block; caught by the byte count rising when it should have fallen, restored from git and redone in one pass.
obs And a third of my own, caught by a rostered guard rather than by care: I put dawk's submodule pin on the operator card, and nib_honesty reads any hash-shaped token there as a claim about THIS repository. bc87acca83 is a commit in dawk's history and resolves nowhere here, so the card advertised a hash that had gone. A foreign pin belongs in the gratitude catalogue, which is where it now lives alone.
loom dawk license=MIT pin=bc87acca83 src_zig_files=42 src_lines=8293 built=yes zig=0.16.0 crux_answer=no
loom peer=pheromone staged=11 blocked=gate3 unshared_rows=2 renumber_to=418,419
file gratitude/dawk the submodule, whole and unmodified
file gratitude/triallax-dawk.md the thanks, and what it taught by refusing
file gratitude/README.md one catalogued entry with source, pin and every vendored license
file tools/l/birth_a_clone.rish four inherited values refused before they are seated, and gpg.program tested executable
file construction/REDS.md %418 -- an inherited value is not a checked value
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- pheromone's eleven files want your hand at the pier shell, and per-tree GNUPGHOME under the jail is a custody call only you can make
